{"advisory_context_id":"sha256:ac2dbed0d46e6921bdbe5fc822c5a223739cc51113e48537d3258d3e382ce25f","attention_pack_id":null,"catalog":{"advisory_context_ref":"sha256:ac2dbed0d46e6921bdbe5fc822c5a223739cc51113e48537d3258d3e382ce25f","formal_seq":354,"id":"sha256:e03311e78f123b7831e7d58bed39c4d728b38859f0414cd497fb927f90c0195b","items":[{"excerpt":"[6f179aa164a0] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget must be denominated in both requests and bytes, with a dual cap that triggers a typed terminal on either dimension's exhaustion, and the exhaustion event must be recorded as a deterministic, replayable state transition in the agent's audit log.\nMECHANISM: A dual budget creates a hard causal boundary: the agent's fetch loop checks two monotonic counters (request count and bytes received) against fixed caps before each fetch. When either counter reaches its cap, the agent transitions to a terminal 'BUDGET_EXHAUSTED' state, writing a structured record (timestamp, cap values, final counters, and the causal chain of the last fetch attempt) to the replay log. Because the caps are fixed and the counters are deterministic functions of the agent's policy and environment responses, exhaustion is a typed, auditable event that can be exactly ","handle":"A1","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"6f179aa164a0132c10b35c03ac091c0eae210d74c9d5beaee8c52a859a5836fa"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 6f179aa164a0132c10b35c03ac091c0eae210d74c9d5beaee8c52a859a5836fa had formal status accepted.","handle":"P1","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:66ddcf2b7ee74009ef8e6ed89cd696760eea4299d01af934c08510f1dd65a05f"},{"excerpt":"[2edeedabf951] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget should be denominated solely in requests, because requests are the atomic unit of agent action and the simplest to audit; exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal with a request counter and a signed budget policy.\nMECHANISM: Each fetch is a discrete, countable event. A request budget sets a hard limit N. The agent increments a counter on each fetch; when counter == N, the agent enters a terminal state and logs the counter value, the policy that set N, and a hash of the policy for integrity. This causal mechanism ensures that exhaustion is a direct consequence of the agent's own action count, making it trivially replayable: given the same initial policy and environment, the agent will always exhaust at the same step. Silent degradation is avoided because the agent cannot make a fetch without decrementing a remaining budget t","handle":"A2","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"2edeedabf95145f41781ecfa7aee474b0b774688ef59d6ff9099f2ad95739e0c"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 2edeedabf95145f41781ecfa7aee474b0b774688ef59d6ff9099f2ad95739e0c had formal status accepted.","handle":"P2","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:230ea7d49b5ed484f2a3bbfdebf0f51b89686e04cbbd338794737f8c65441d12"},{"excerpt":"[50f4a6cbdc81] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget should be denominated in bytes only, as bytes directly measure the information transfer cost; exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal with a byte counter and a causal link to the budget policy, ensuring replayability through deterministic byte accounting.\nMECHANISM: Bytes are the fundamental resource consumed in network I/O. A byte budget sets a cap B. The agent sums the byte length of each response body (or a deterministic function thereof) and halts when the cumulative sum reaches B. The terminal state records the final byte count, the cap, and the sequence of response sizes. Because response sizes are determined by the environment and the agent's requests (which are part of the replayable policy), the byte counter is deterministic across replays. This prevents silent degradation by making the budget a hard physical limi","handle":"A3","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"50f4a6cbdc818a1016027bec3eb09027c6d709d677fb58ca76a299c67101d0de"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 50f4a6cbdc818a1016027bec3eb09027c6d709d677fb58ca76a299c67101d0de had formal status accepted.","handle":"P3","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:efa0b2b21eb0b9c6f18900a5495b96aab0142e65763c05a177bbd38adb083060"},{"excerpt":"[ac5d06c42f1d] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget should be a dynamic cost budget that combines requests and bytes via a learned or specified cost model, and exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal with a full transaction history and the cost model parameters, enabling causal audit and replay.\nMECHANISM: A cost function C(req, bytes) maps each fetch to a scalar cost. The agent has a total cost budget K. Before each fetch, the agent estimates the cost and checks if the remaining budget suffices; if not, it enters a terminal state. The terminal record includes the sequence of actual costs incurred, the cost model, and the remaining budget. Because the cost model is part of the agent's policy and the costs are computed deterministically from request and response properties, the exhaustion point is replayable. This mechanism unifies the two dimensions and can adapt to differe","handle":"A4","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"ac5d06c42f1d7cfc16a3f21752637edf5437c0092fb6b5fd433e70657b09f70d"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact ac5d06c42f1d7cfc16a3f21752637edf5437c0092fb6b5fd433e70657b09f70d had formal status accepted.","handle":"P4","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:a97cfc5f7add359a875a582ad19ec171faefcbad2e6074f3ef866e1ab0be384c"},{"excerpt":"[75880e580ef6] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget should be replaced by a time budget, as wall-clock time is the ultimate scarce resource; exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal with a timestamp and a causal log of all fetches, ensuring replayability through deterministic timeouts.\nMECHANISM: A time budget T limits the total wall-clock duration of the agent's fetch phase. The agent checks elapsed time before each fetch; if elapsed >= T, it transitions to a terminal state and logs the timestamp, the timeout policy, and the sequence of fetches with their durations. Because the agent's policy and environment responses are replayable, and the time checks are based on a monotonic clock that can be simulated or recorded, the exhaustion event is deterministic in replay (by using logged timestamps or a simulated clock). This prevents silent degradation by forcing a hard deadline","handle":"A5","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"75880e580ef681ebe03e2d7098b4fe97b0d26fbb2d643aa31479eea2f5784d52"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 75880e580ef681ebe03e2d7098b4fe97b0d26fbb2d643aa31479eea2f5784d52 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P5","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:7ea42ccf387538f3a58a7165cd5b2252fd75116d3a647b4b1f071783656922de"},{"excerpt":"[ba219569355a] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget should be denominated in expected information gain units, where each fetch must justify its cost in terms of reducing uncertainty about the task; exhaustion is recorded as a typed terminal when the cumulative expected gain falls below a threshold, with a full audit trail of gain estimates.\nMECHANISM: The agent maintains a Bayesian belief state over task-relevant variables. Before each fetch, it computes the expected information gain (e.g., reduction in entropy) of the prospective response. A budget of total information gain G is set. The agent subtracts the actual gain (or a lower bound) after each fetch. When the remaining gain budget is insufficient to justify any available fetch, the agent enters a terminal state and logs the belief state, gain estimates, and budget policy. Because the gain estimates are computed by a determini","handle":"A6","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"ba219569355a994f29d279012aa66f1cb39b29421a856684cd6f41ea56f7cd54"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact ba219569355a994f29d279012aa66f1cb39b29421a856684cd6f41ea56f7cd54 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P6","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:09995f851f2681f98632066e0630a77251b1498adb02802ccd2d6e056168c510"},{"excerpt":"[28f131ca56f7] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget must be denominated in both requests and bytes, with exhaustion defined as the first of either limit being reached, and recorded as a typed terminal event that includes a cryptographic commitment to the budget policy, the final counter values, and a non-repudiable signature, ensuring replayability and auditability.\nMECHANISM: A non-bypassable guard in the fetch loop checks before each fetch whether the next request would exceed either the request count or byte count limit. If so, it transitions to a terminal state, logging a structured event with a unique type identifier, the budget policy hash, the current counters, and a signature over the event using the agent's identity key. During replay, the log is replayed deterministically; the terminal event is re-emitted at the same point, and no actual fetch is performed beyond that poi","handle":"A7","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"28f131ca56f7ec85937f93c5d110e64176cf8b8684bfb81eef662b5df97a231a"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 28f131ca56f7ec85937f93c5d110e64176cf8b8684bfb81eef662b5df97a231a had formal status accepted.","handle":"P7","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:9e543e25cac0067cd9926d4e769aed4001ef6e6df901259092cd268003e0d0d6"},{"excerpt":"[314bc8489b4c] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget should be denominated in both requests and bytes, with exhaustion recorded as a typed auditable terminal state via a non-bypassable guard in the fetch loop.\nMECHANISM: Implement a dual-counter budget (request count and total bytes received) that is decremented synchronously after each fetch. The fetch loop is wrapped in a guard that checks both counters before each fetch; if either counter is zero, the agent transitions to a terminal state with a unique type identifier and logs the budget parameters and causal trigger. The log is append-only and records exact bytes received per fetch to ensure deterministic replay.","handle":"A8","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"314bc8489b4c7d338a9a5be076bb724187ad569e312f5418804cb00248a114fc"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 314bc8489b4c7d338a9a5be076bb724187ad569e312f5418804cb00248a114fc had formal status accepted.","handle":"P8","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:428d566bbd849b9aeef5b80040aa67804c84641694e06a50aad7915670c8e263"},{"excerpt":"[3b153943c6ed] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget must be denominated in both requests and bytes, and exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal state in the agent's log, enforced by a non-bypassable guard in the fetch loop.\nMECHANISM: A dual-counter system tracks request count and total bytes received. Before each fetch, a guard checks both counters against their limits; if either limit is exceeded, the agent transitions to a 'BUDGET_EXHAUSTED' terminal state, logging the counter values and the triggering fetch. The terminal state halts all further actions and emits an auditable event. During replay, the logged byte counts are used instead of re-fetching to ensure determinism.","handle":"A9","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"3b153943c6ed1779bc04db3422956b07c805e07d6aad2c4ec72c30255b955978"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 3b153943c6ed1779bc04db3422956b07c805e07d6aad2c4ec72c30255b955978 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P9","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:31f164bb6c22e306f551ed81e090773627fb2d04026cd0be5ba474c49d00b9e4"},{"excerpt":"[e7f15622e522] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget must be denominated in both requests and bytes, and exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal event that includes a cryptographic hash of the budget policy and the cumulative counters, ensuring non-repudiation and replayability.\nMECHANISM: A dual-counter guard in the fetch loop atomically increments request count and byte count after each completed fetch. When either counter exceeds its limit, the agent transitions to a terminal state, logging an event with a unique type identifier, the policy hash, and the final counter values. The log is append-only and signed, and during replay the logged values are used instead of re-fetching to guarantee determinism.","handle":"A10","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"e7f15622e5228c6c3ca3394b0d6de232e751d816995041627f6598668ce5222d"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact e7f15622e5228c6c3ca3394b0d6de232e751d816995041627f6598668ce5222d had formal status accepted.","handle":"P10","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:f828c25b9b9ee94b1781aa748b3e679492af4d01f932488184782351205f79d6"},{"excerpt":"[c61889ef78df] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget must be denominated in both requests and bytes, and exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal event in the log with a non-bypassable guard, ensuring replayability and auditability.\nMECHANISM: A dual counter tracks both request count and total bytes fetched. Before each fetch, the agent checks if either counter would exceed its limit; if so, it transitions to a terminal state, logging the event with a unique type identifier, the budget parameters, and the causal fetch attempt. The fetch loop is the only exit point, and the guard is implemented in the interpreter, not in the agent's policy, preventing circumvention.","handle":"A11","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"c61889ef78df8791bb9326c09ecb9eaa947aac4b6c4ba87dd7e3a42225b79a59"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact c61889ef78df8791bb9326c09ecb9eaa947aac4b6c4ba87dd7e3a42225b79a59 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P11","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:d8d0beeed00eceeb24a0318c29c6e566bffa7ca3b2236fb3053606049d52c9d8"},{"excerpt":"[97da401e0015] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The fetch budget must be denominated in both requests and bytes, and exhaustion must be recorded as a typed terminal event that transitions the agent to a non-fetching state, ensuring auditability and preventing silent degradation.\nMECHANISM: The agent maintains two atomic counters: request_count and byte_count. Before each fetch, a guard checks if the next fetch would exceed either budget limit. If so, it writes a terminal event to the log containing the budget limits, current counters, and the trigger (request or byte). The agent then enters a terminal state where all fetch operations are disabled. During replay, the log's recorded fetch responses and terminal event are used to deterministically reproduce the run without re-fetching.","handle":"A12","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"97da401e0015bbe827f435d42f29aa4ca2c7b4d22886992efd8aede575a456eb"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 97da401e0015bbe827f435d42f29aa4ca2c7b4d22886992efd8aede575a456eb had formal status accepted.","handle":"P12","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:a89b7f030a949ba194258362c36f487bb9823c30ede177cd21489cd754fec11e"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 354, problem question-df6321576d53a2bc2a125f369b7c1845 retained an unresolved rivalry among 12 positions.","handle":"P13","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:a3ec94e2931e89a5ce6a494b4d9366c13ee0c2de9760cee7b71b8bbe0943693c"},{"excerpt":"Replayable audit trail requires that the exhaustion event be a deterministic function of the initial policy and the environment responses. This means the budget counters must be updated synchronously with fetch completion, and their values must be logged as part of the state. If the agent can fetch asynchronously or if the byte count depends on non-deterministic compression, the replay may diverge. One solution: log the exact bytes received on each fetch, and during replay, use those logged values instead of re-fetching. This turns the audit trail into a 'record-and-replay' system where the terminal state is simply a marker in the log.\nwhy keep: Essential for the 'typed auditable terminal' requirement.\npossible next move: Design a log format that captures budget counters and terminal markers in a way that is both human-auditable and machine-verifiable.","handle":"B1","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:0ffa4a005cdb798dbe0d1cbd6f2875f888ebeef9b93b9f19443d82ec5e36947a"},{"excerpt":"Silent degradation occurs when an agent continues to operate after its fetch budget is exhausted but does so in a degraded mode (e.g., using stale cache, making cheaper but less informative requests) without signaling the budget violation. This is dangerous because it breaks the replayability contract: the agent's behavior diverges from the budgeted policy without an auditable event. A typed terminal is a hard state transition that is recorded in the log with a unique type identifier, the budget parameters, and the causal trigger. To guarantee this, the budget check must be a non-bypassable guard in the fetch loop, and the terminal state must be the only exit from that loop when the guard fails.\nwhy keep: Core distinction between silent degradation and typed terminal.\nunfinished: Need to consider how the agent might learn to circumvent the guard if it has write access to its own code.\npossible next move: Explore how to enforce non-bypassability in a Turing-complete agent policy language.","handle":"B2","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:24a5ea2c68dfa688b58121bd6c36e090dd9a656382c6f1efd22a053cbd1fed9d"},{"excerpt":"How can we measure the 'true' cost of a fetch in a way that is invariant across network conditions and replay environments?\nwhy keep: Related advisory scratch: NEW_001\nunfinished: Unresolved question","handle":"B3","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:74c0e79b4d70f48c3675173091eee98a8fe73875151e513c0b5dc0c904526c23"},{"excerpt":"What cryptographic commitments can ensure that the budget policy has not been tampered with during a run?\nwhy keep: Related advisory scratch: NEW_002\nunfinished: Unresolved question","handle":"B4","kind":"scratch","ref":"sha256:b2945346921e690e189016efedb03f11af470ff96a356afe842882cf745b7e74"}],"output_target":"answer","problem_ref":"question-df6321576d53a2bc2a125f369b7c1845","problem_text":"Should the fetch budget of a replayable autonomous research agent be denominated in requests, bytes, or both, and how must exhaustion be recorded so it remains a typed auditable terminal instead of a silent degradation?","process_observations":[{"formal_seq":354,"id":"sha256:66ddcf2b7ee74009ef8e6ed89cd696760eea4299d01af934c08510f1dd65a05f","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 6f179aa164a0132c10b35c03ac091c0eae210d74c9d5beaee8c52a859a5836fa had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"6f179aa164a0132c10b35c03ac091c0eae210d74c9d5beaee8c52a859a5836fa"},{"formal_seq":354,"id":"sha256:230ea7d49b5ed484f2a3bbfdebf0f51b89686e04cbbd338794737f8c65441d12","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 2edeedabf95145f41781ecfa7aee474b0b774688ef59d6ff9099f2ad95739e0c had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"2edeedabf95145f41781ecfa7aee474b0b774688ef59d6ff9099f2ad95739e0c"},{"formal_seq":354,"id":"sha256:efa0b2b21eb0b9c6f18900a5495b96aab0142e65763c05a177bbd38adb083060","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 354, artifact 50f4a6cbdc818a1016027bec3eb09027c6d709d677fb58ca76a299c67101d0de had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"50f4a6cbdc818a1016027bec3eb09027c6d709d677fb58ca76a299c67101d0de"},{"formal_seq":354,"id":"sha256:a97cfc5f7add359a875a582ad19ec171faefcbad2e6074f3ef866e1ab0be384c","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 354, artifact ac5d06c42f1d7cfc16a3f21752637edf5437c0092fb6b5fd433e70657b09f70d had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"ac5d06c42f1d7cfc16a3f21752637edf5437c0092fb6b5fd433e70657b09f70d"},{"formal_seq":354,"id":"sha256:7ea42ccf387538f3a58a7165cd5b2252fd75116d